belong	verb intransitive1. To be the property of; as, a field belongs to Richard Roe; Jamaica belongs to G.Britain.2. To be the concern or proper business of; to appertain; as, it belongs to John Doe to prove his title.3. To be appendant to.He went into a desert place belonging to Bethsaida. Luke 9:1.4. To be a part of, or connected with, though detached in place; as, a beam or rafter belongs to such a frame, or to such a place in the building.5. To have relation to.And David said, to whom belongest thou? 1.Sam.30.6. To be the quality or attribute of.To the Lord our God belong mercies and forgiveness. Daniel 9:9.7. To be suitable for.Strong meat belongeth to them of full age. Hebrews 5:14.8. To relate to, or be referred to.He careth for things that belong to the Lord. l Cor.7.9. To have a legal residence, settlement, or inhabitancy, whether by birth or operation of law, so as to be entitled to maintenance by the parish or town.Bastards also are settled in the parishes to which the mothers belong Hence,10. To be the native of; to have original residence.There is no other country in the world to which the Gipeys could belong11. In common language, to have a settled residence; to be domiciliated.
