deceivableness	noun1. Liableness to be deceived.2. Liableness to deceive.The deceivableness of unrighteousness. 2 Thessalonians 2:10.
deceive	verb transitive [L to take asid, to ensnare.]1. To mislead the mind; to cause to err; to cause to believe what is false, or disbelieve what is true; to impose on; to delude.Take heed that no man deceive you. Matthew 24:4.If we say we have no sin, we deceive ourselves. 1 John 1:1.2. To beguile; to cheat.Your father hath deceived me, and changed my wages ten times.3. To cut off from expectation; to frustrate or disappoint; as, his hopes were deceived.4. To take from; to rob.Plant fruit trees in large borders, and set therein fine flowers, but thin and sparingly, lest they deceive the trees.
deceived	participle passive Misled; led into error; beguiled; cheated; deluded.
deceiver	noun One who deceives; one who leads into error; a cheat; an impostor.I shall seem to my father as a deceiver Genesis 27:12.
deceivers	noun One who deceives; one who leads into error; a cheat; an impostor.I shall seem to my father as a deceiver Genesis 27:12.
