esteem	verb transitive [Latin estimo; Gr. to honor or esteem ]1. To set a value on, whether high or low; to estimate; to value.Then he forsook God who made him, and lightly esteemed the rock of his salvation. Deuteronomy 32:15.They that despise me shall be lightly esteemed. 1 Samuel 2:30.2. To prize; to set a high value on; to regard with reverence, respect or friendship. When our minds are not biased, we always esteem the industrious, the generous, the brave, the virtuous, and the learned.Will he esteem thy riches? Job 36:19.3. To hold in opinion; to repute; to think.One man esteemeth one day above another; another esteemeth every day alike. Romans 14:5.4. To compare in value; to estimate by proportion. [Little used.]ESTEE'M, noun Estimation; opinion or judgment of merit or demerit. This man is of no worth in my esteem1. High value or estimation; great regard; favorable opinion, founded on supposed worth.Both those poets lived in much esteem with good and holy men in orders.
