levy	verb transitive [Latin levo; Eng. to lift.]1. To raise; to collect. To levy troops, is to enlist or to order men into public service. To levy an army, is to collect troops and form an army by enrollment, conscription or other means.2. To raise; to collect by assessment; as, to levy taxes, toll tribute, or contributions.To levy war, is to raise or begin war; to take arms for attack; to attack.To levy a fine, to commence and carry on a suit for assuring the title to lands or tenements.LEV'Y, noun1. The act of collecting men for military, or other public service, as by enlistment, enrollment or other means. 1 Kings 9:15.2. Troops collected; an army raised. 1 Kings 5:13.3. The act of collecting money for public use by tax or other imposition.4. War raised. [Not in use.]
