room	noun1. Space; compass; extent of place, great or small. Let the words occupy as little room as possible.2. Space or place unoccupied.Lord, it is done as thou hast commanded, and yet there is room Luke 14:8.3. Place for reception or admission of any thing. In this case, there is no room for doubt or for argument.4. Place of another; stead; as in succession or substitution. One magistrate or king comes in the room of a former one. We often place one thing in the room of another. 1 Kings 20:24.5. Unoccupied opportunity. The eager pursuit of wealth leaves little room for serious reflection.6. An apartment in a house; any division separated from the rest by a partition; as a parlor, drawing room or bed-room; also an apartment in a ship, as the cook-room, bread-room, gun-room, etc.7. A seat. Luke 14:8.To make room to open a way or passage; to free from obstructions.To make room to open a space or place for any thing.To give room to withdraw; to leave space unoccupied for others to pass or to be seated.ROOM, verb intransitive To occupy an apartment; to lodge; an academic use of the word. A B rooms at No. 7.
