thunder	noun [Latin tonitru, from tono, to sound.]1. The sound which follows an explosion of electricity or lightning; the report of a discharge of electrical fluid, that is, of its passage from one cloud to another, or from a cloud to the earth, or from the earth to a cloud. When this explosion is near to a person, the thunder is a rattling or clattering sound, and when distant, the sound is heavy and rumbling. The fact is in some degree the same with the report of a cannon. This sharpness or acuteness of the sound when near, and the rumbling murmur when distant, are the principal distinctions in thunder [Thunder is not lightning, but the effect of it. See Johnson's Dictionary, under thunder ]There were thunders and lightnings. Exodus 19:16.2. thunder is used for lightning, or for a thunderbolt, either originally through ignorance, or by way of metaphor, or because the lightning and thunder are closely united.The revenging gods'Gainst parricides all the thunder bend.3. Any loud noise; as the thunder of cannon.Sons of thunder Mark 3:17.4. Denunciation published; as the thunders of the Vatican.THUN'DER, verb intransitive To sound, rattle or roar, as an explosion of electricity.Canst thou thunder with a voice like him? Job 40:9.1. To make a loud noise, particularly a heavy sound of some continuance.His dreadful voice no moreWould thunder in my ears.2. To rattle, or give a heavy rattling sound.And roll the thund'ring chariot o'er the ground.THUN'DER, verb transitive To emit with noise and terror.Oracles severeWere daily thunder'd in our gen'ral's ear.1. To publish any denunciation or threat.An archdeacon, as being a prelate, may thunder out an ecclesiastical censure.
