widows	noun [Latin See Wide.] A woman who has lost her husband by death. Luke 2:37.WIDOWs chamber, in London, the apparel and furniture of the bed-chamber of the widow of a freeman, to which she is entitled.WIDOW, verb transitive1. To bereave of a husband; but rarely used except in the participle.2. To endow with a widows right. [Unusual.]3. To strip of any thing good.The widowd isle in mourning--
wife	noun plural Wives. [G., a woman.]1. The lawful consort of man; a woman who is united to man in the lawful bonds of wedlock; the correlative of husband.The husband of one wife 1 Timothy 3:2.Let every one of you in particular, so love his wife even as himself, and let the wife see that she reverence her husband. Ephesians 5:23.2. A woman of low employment; as strawberry wives. [Not in use.]
